NAME
    echo - print arguments to stdout

SYNOPSIS
    echo [ARG...]

DESCRIPTION
    Writes each ARG to stdout separated by spaces, followed by a
    newline. Useful for shell scripts and inside pipelines as a
    constant string source.

    The shell's variable expansion happens BEFORE echo sees its
    argv — `echo $PATH` is shell-expanded to `echo /` (or wherever
    PATH is) before the binary runs. echo itself just prints what
    it's given.

EXAMPLES
    echo hello world
    echo $PS1                           # current prompt
    echo "spaces are fine"              # quotes are shell, not echo
    echo line1 > out.txt                # redirect to file

EXIT STATUS
    0   always

SEE ALSO
    sh(1), cat(1)

NOTES
    No -n (suppress newline) yet. No -e (interpret escapes). The
    output is always exactly the argv joined by single spaces + \n.
